	@media (min-width: 768px) {
		.project-grid {
			/*
			 * `auto` sizes the middle track to exactly `.project`'s own
			 * max-width (grid caps an `auto` track's contribution at the
			 * item's max-width). Whenever there's enough room, both flanking
			 * tracks still split the leftover space evenly (plain `1fr` vs
			 * `1fr` always does, regardless of their differing minimums below)
			 * — so wide viewports stay symmetric with no separate breakpoint
			 * needed to "switch on" centering, the same `1fr auto 1fr` pattern
			 * rachelchen.tech's project pages use.
			 *
			 * The two minimums differ on purpose: once the viewport gets tight
			 * enough that an even split would dip the LEFT (sidebar) track
			 * below 160px, the RIGHT track — pure whitespace, nothing placed
			 * in it — keeps shrinking past that point on its own (down to its
			 * own 48px floor) while the left one holds at 160. That soaks up
			 * the squeeze as shrinking right-side padding first, keeping the
			 * sidebar and `.project`'s reading width intact much longer than a
			 * symmetric `minmax(160px, 1fr)` on both sides would.
			 *
			 * 160/48/768 are measured off rachelchen.tech's rendered project
			 * page, not read off its Tailwind classes — its `lg:max-w-4xl`
			 * never resolves, so the column caps at 768px at every width.
			 * The floors are explicit here because our TOC links are full `##`
			 * heading text: an `auto` minimum would track the longest word
			 * instead of holding a fixed width.
			 */
			grid-template-columns: minmax(160px, 1fr) auto minmax(48px, 1fr);
			column-gap: 2rem;
		}
		.side {
			grid-column: 1;
			position: sticky;
			/*
			 * Must equal the sidebar's own resting offset, or the difference
			 * shows up as a lurch on the first pixels of scroll — it pinned at
			 * 4.5rem while resting at 86px, so every TOC click from the top of
			 * the page snapped it 14px upward before it locked.
			 *
			 * Resting offset = the sticky nav's height + this grid's top
			 * padding. Nav.astro's box is 1rem padding + one line of body text
			 * + 1rem padding + a 1px rule; `em` is used for the line so this
			 * tracks the body font-size step at the 1020px breakpoint instead
			 * of drifting 1px below it.
			 */
			top: calc(2rem + 1.4em + 1px + 2rem);
			height: fit-content;
		}
		.project {
			grid-column: 2;
			/*
			 * `max-width` caps line length for readability, but the `auto`
			 * track above is sized to exactly this max-width (grid caps an
			 * `auto` track's contribution at the item's max-width) — without
			 * auto margins, the capped column hugs the track's left edge
			 * instead of centering in it, which reads as the whole column
			 * leaning left. Scoped to this breakpoint (not the base `.project`
			 * rule): a grid item with auto margins loses stretch alignment and
			 * shrink-wraps to its content's intrinsic width instead of the
			 * track's, which blew mobile out to the CLI case study's ~680px
			 * unscaled terminal width regardless of viewport.
